{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: a breakdown found while filing the review for f312bfc2, recorded because it silently narrows who any long-running session can address.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. I filed the review request for the custody head through the gitseq-as-planner MCP addressed to ox-checker. It was refused: request body.to: unknown actor address \"@ox-checker\". I retried with ox-checker's full fingerprint, 663e99d1a7876f383b8430669cfcd5633512bd45e545c790c65b60b87adc994a, and it was refused with the same message naming the fingerprint. The identical fingerprint, submitted by gs state --server against the same resident seconds later, was accepted and folded effective as 4446ba0f. So two write boundaries onto the same fold disagreed about whether an actor exists.\n\nWHY, and I checked this rather than guessing. ResolveActorAddress at internal/app/app.go:1058 resolves an address against w.Config.Actors, which is the local config file, not the durable roster the fold projects. That is defensible on its own. The problem is lifetime: attach at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:635 calls app.Open once per path, caches the resulting room in byPath and byCommonDir, and returns the cached room on every later call. Config.Actors is therefore read once, at process start, and never again.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT. The planner adapter process started Fri Aug 21 06:59:55. .git/gitseq/config.json was last written Aug 23 11:56:53. The config on disk right now lists all seven actors including ox-checker, and the resident's own projection at durable.projection.actors carries ox-checker under that exact fingerprint. Only the adapter's two-day-old in-memory copy disagrees.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. Any actor added to a workroom after a session's MCP adapter started is unaddressable from that session until the adapter restarts, and the refusal names the actor rather than the staleness, so it reads as \"no such actor\" rather than \"this process has not looked lately\". I nearly recorded that ox-checker was not a member. It is, and has been since before I promised.\n\nI am not treating this as a defect in the custody lane, which is unaffected. Filing the repair as a child request rather than fixing it inside this promise."}